-- ============================================================================
-- PD9 ASSOCIATE DEEP SEED: Production Surveillance, beginner tier.
--
-- Six modules, 26 lessons, 132 questions (15 per module bank plus a 42
-- question final exam). The structure row is GENERATED from the tier's
-- manifest.json rather than hand typed.
--
-- What this tier owns: THE LEDGER, THE PRODUCING-DAY RATE, THE RATIOS A ROW
-- CARRIES, AND THE ROLL-UP TO A FIELD. Two of its six graded fields come out
-- of `derivePoint` called on ONE ledger row, and the other four are the four
-- continuous members of ONE `computeKpis` return. Both functions take data and
-- give back numbers with NO baseline, NO second window, NO comparison and NO
-- verdict anywhere near them: `derivePoint` sees one row and nothing else, and
-- `computeKpis` sees a trailing window and forms means and ratios of means. So
-- the tier is exactly the part of the domain that has no second window in it,
-- AND THE TIER ENDS WHERE THE COMPARISON ARRIVES.
--
-- THE SPLIT IS IN THE CODE AND NOT IN A SYLLABUS. `surveillance.js` divides
-- cleanly into functions that read rows and functions that compare windows.
-- `derivePoint`, `buildWellSeries`, `buildFieldSeries` and `computeKpis` are
-- the whole of the first group and they are the whole of this tier.
-- `detectExceptions` is the first function in the file that holds two windows
-- at once, and everything it decides is above this tier by construction. A
-- learner who finishes here can turn a ledger row into rates and ratios, say
-- which of the fourteen keys on a point is COPIED and which is COMPUTED, name
-- the exact condition each computed one refuses on, and roll a field up. That
-- is a real and complete skill, and it is also the baseline the two tiers
-- above then take apart.
--
-- THE ONE SENTENCE THE WHOLE COURSE RESTS ON, AND IT IS SAID HERE FIRST
-- BECAUSE THIS IS THE TIER WHERE BOTH QUANTITIES ARE STILL IN FRONT OF YOU ON
-- THE SAME ROW. A CALENDAR VOLUME AND A PRODUCING-DAY RATE ARE DIFFERENT
-- QUANTITIES, AND ON THE TEACHING WELL THEY MOVE IN OPPOSITE DIRECTIONS. A
-- ledger row books a VOLUME against a calendar day and, if somebody recorded
-- it, the HOURS the well was on. `derivePoint` keeps both and then computes
-- `oilPd`, `waterPd`, `gasPd` and `liquidPd`, the volume scaled to twenty-four
-- hours, which the module's own header calls "the number that says how the
-- well is performing as opposed to how much it made". On OGUTA-6 the
-- producing-day oil rate is 512.000000000 stb/d on every one of the seven
-- recent days, flat to the last digit, while the calendar volume falls from
-- 502.666666666667 stb to 307.504761904762 stb. Read one way that well lost
-- 38.825312618416 per cent. Read the other it GAINED 1.856763925729 per cent.
-- Neither number is wrong and neither is the other one, and a learner who
-- leaves this tier unable to say which column a figure came from cannot read a
-- single result in the two tiers above.
--
-- UNITS ARE NOT A HOUSE STYLE HERE, THEY ARE THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN THE TWO
-- QUANTITIES ABOVE. A ledger volume is a VOLUME OVER A ROW and is written stb
-- or Mscf. A producing-day rate is a RATE and is written stb/d or Mscf/d.
-- Hours are h. A watercut is a FRACTION in surveillance and in allocation, and
-- it is a PER CENT in the two lift modules, so every figure says which. A
-- gas-oil ratio is scf/stb, which is why the thousand is in the formula at
-- all. And nothing in this domain converts a unit for you: field units go in
-- and field units come out.
--
-- FIVE THINGS THE TIER MUST NOT LET A LEARNER ASSUME.
--
--   A RATIO REFUSES ON A DIFFERENT CONDITION FROM A RATE, AND THE TWO
--   CONDITIONS ARE NOT THE SAME COLUMN. `watercut` is set only where the
--   LIQUID is above zero, so a row with no liquid has no watercut. `gor` is
--   set only where the OIL is above zero, so a row with no oil has no gas-oil
--   ratio however much gas it made, and a gas well filing a gas-only row gets
--   a null for the one ratio it exists to report. A NEGATIVE volume, which is
--   how a ledger books a back-out, is not refused at all: it is arithmetic,
--   and it gives a negative liquid, a null watercut and an ordinary-looking
--   gas-oil ratio. Three different behaviours out of one function, and only
--   one of them is a refusal.
--
--   ZERO HOURS IS A REFUSAL AND A MISSING HOURS COLUMN IS NOT. `derivePoint`
--   reads zero hours as SHUT IN and returns a producing-day rate of null
--   rather than an infinity, which the module header calls the single most
--   important refusal in the file, because an infinity propagates into every
--   mean downstream and turns a shut-in day into a fabricated record rate. An
--   ABSENT hours column is a different thing: uptime is unknown, and the
--   producing-day rate is set EQUAL to the calendar volume. So a well that
--   never records its hours has four producing-day columns that are numerically
--   its calendar columns and are not claims about uptime at all. The tier above
--   meets the same silence read the opposite way in a different file.
--
--   A FIELD TOTAL IS NOT THE SUM OF THE WELL SERIES, AND NEITHER RETURN SAYS
--   SO. `buildWellSeries` silently DROPS every row that carries no well id;
--   `buildFieldSeries` keeps it. Two rows in, one of them with no well, gives
--   one well series holding one point and a field total holding both. The
--   field series also decides a well is producing with
--   (oil || 0) + (water || 0) + (gas || 0) > 0, which adds stb to Mscf to make
--   one boolean. It is harmless as a boolean and it is the only place in the
--   whole domain where a barrel and a thousand cubic feet are summed, and a
--   learner should be able to say why that is a joke rather than a bug.
--
--   `computeKpis` GUARDS THE LIQUID AGAINST A NULL MEAN AND DOES NOT GUARD THE
--   WATERCUT ON THE VERY NEXT LINE. `liquid` is written as oil and water added
--   only when NEITHER is null; `watercut` is written as water over oil plus
--   water with no such test, and null plus a number is a number in the
--   comparison that follows. So a field series whose oil mean comes back null
--   and whose water mean does not reports NO liquid and a watercut of EXACTLY
--   ONE, side by side, in the same object. One line apart, two different
--   opinions about whether the answer exists.
--
--   AND WHO IS IN A COUNT IS NOT WHO YOU THINK. `wellCount` is every series
--   handed in, injectors and observation wells included. `producerCount`
--   excludes ONLY injectors, so an observation well is counted as a producer
--   by that name. And the uptime skips injectors and reads everything else, so
--   an observation well recording a perfect twenty-four hours a day and
--   producing nothing is averaged in with the real wells: on the teaching
--   field that is 92.011904762 per cent with it against 90.014880952 per cent
--   without. The number moves in the flattering direction, which is the
--   direction nobody checks.
--
-- WHAT THE ORACLE CHECKS, SAID HERE AND WITHOUT HEDGING, BECAUSE THIS TIER IS
-- WHERE IT CHECKS THE MOST. `oracle_surveillance.py` is a genuinely
-- independent referee and not a transcription: it does every date arithmetic
-- on the CALENDAR where the module counts epoch-millisecond day numbers; it
-- forms every window mean by explicit calendar membership from the STATED
-- window definition rather than from the implemented inequality; it measures
-- effective decline as 1 - q(365)/q(0) through the Arps rate law where the
-- module evaluates a closed form; and it gates the decline fit against a
-- series SYNTHESISED from known parameters, so the truth is known by
-- construction and no reimplementation of the fitter is involved anywhere.
-- Saying that clearly, first, is what makes the Expert tier's account of what
-- the oracle CANNOT see a finding rather than a grievance.
--
-- THE HABIT THIS TIER STARTS, AND IT IS WHAT THE COURSE IS ACTUALLY FOR.
-- ALWAYS SAY WHICH COLUMN. Every figure this tier produces is quoted with the
-- column it was read from and the row or window it was read over, on the cases
-- where that is obvious and nothing turns on it. Doing it here, where it costs
-- nothing, is what makes the sentence mean something two tiers later, where a
-- printed severity depends on it.
--
-- The only numbers that travel from this tier to the next are the field's own
-- stated conditions: the ledger rows, the wells and their types, the tests,
-- the metered totals and the settings. The tier above restates every one of
-- them from scratch and forms its own windows.
-- ============================================================================

insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
values ('surveillance', 'beginner', '{"modules":[{"key":"m01-a-ledger-and-a-question","title":"A Ledger and a Question","lesson_keys":["l01-what-surveillance-is-for","l02-four-modules-and-one-idea","l03-what-a-row-holds","l04-what-these-engines-refuse"]},{"key":"m02-the-producing-day-rate","title":"The Producing-Day Rate","lesson_keys":["l01-a-volume-is-not-a-rate","l02-scaling-to-twenty-four-hours","l03-zero-hours-is-a-refusal","l04-hours-that-are-not-a-number","l05-what-the-rate-does-not-say"]},{"key":"m03-the-ratios-a-row-carries","title":"The Ratios a Row Carries","lesson_keys":["l01-watercut-is-a-fraction-of-liquid","l02-the-gas-oil-ratio-is-per-barrel-of-oil","l03-where-each-ratio-refuses","l04-a-ratio-is-a-reading-of-a-period"]},{"key":"m04-from-rows-to-a-field","title":"From Rows to a Field","lesson_keys":["l01-grouping-a-ledger-by-well","l02-the-field-day","l03-what-counts-as-a-well-on-stream","l04-cadence-and-the-moving-average","l05-the-decimator-and-its-cap"]},{"key":"m05-the-field-kpis","title":"The Field KPIs","lesson_keys":["l01-a-trailing-window-of-days","l02-mean-rates-and-the-ratios-of-means","l03-uptime-and-who-is-in-it","l04-two-counts-and-what-they-count","l05-what-a-kpi-cannot-tell-you"]},{"key":"m06-the-associate-reading","title":"The Associate Reading","lesson_keys":["l01-the-story-so-far","l02-working-the-capstone","l03-onward"]}]}'::jsonb, 1)
on conflict (app_slug, tier) do update
  set structure = excluded.structure,
      content_version = excluded.content_version,
      active = true,
      updated_at = now();

delete from public.academy_quiz_questions
 where app_slug = 'surveillance' and tier = 'beginner';

insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-a-ledger-and-a-question', 1, 'Nothing in surveillance.js measures a well. What is the strongest claim the module is built to support?', '["That a well is underperforming its neighbours on the same field, which is why the return sorts the wells against each other before printing them.", "That a well is bad, which is the verdict a severity of high is the printed form of.", "That a well changed against a reading of itself over an earlier window.", "That a well made 100.000000000000 stb/d when its baseline says it should have made 300.000000000000 stb/d."]'::jsonb, 2, 'detectExceptions compares a recent window against a baseline window on the SAME well, so the output is always that this well changed and never that this well is bad.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-a-ledger-and-a-question', 2, 'detectExceptions anchors every window on what?', '["The field''s latest ledger date, which the return states as asOf.", "The wall clock at the moment the call runs, which is why a stale dataset flags every well.", "The first ledger date in the field, counted forward by recentDays and then by baselineDays.", "The earliest date on the well being surveilled, taken per well so that two wells on one field never share an anchor."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The published case returns asOf = 2025-06-30, the field''s own latest ledger date, so a three-year-old dataset surveils honestly instead of declaring every well stale.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-a-ledger-and-a-question', 3, 'At the default settings on the published field, how are the recent and the baseline window laid out?', '["They share the 7 days of the recent window, since the baseline is the same rows read over a longer run.", "The baseline runs forward 30 days from the first ledger date and the recent window is the last 7 of them.", "The recent window closes on 2025-06-23 and the baseline opens on it, so the boundary day is counted twice.", "The recent window is the 7 days 2025-06-24 to 2025-06-30 and the baseline is the 30 days 2025-05-25 to 2025-06-23, both half open, so the boundary day belongs to the earlier of the two."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Both windows are half open and do not overlap, so a boundary day belongs to exactly one of them and it is the earlier.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-a-ledger-and-a-question', 4, 'derivePoint returns fourteen keys off one ledger row. How do they divide?', '["Nine are computed and five are copied through, which is why a row with a missing column still returns a full object with a value on every key it computed.", "Nine are copied through unchanged and five are computed, and every one of the five can be null.", "All fourteen are computed from the row, and the four producing-day rates are the only ones that can come back null.", "Five are copied and nine computed."]'::jsonb, 1, 'liquid, watercut, gor and the four producing-day rates are the computed side, and each of those can refuse.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-a-ledger-and-a-question', 5, 'Three of the five published rows return a gor of 500.000000000 scf/stb at three different oil volumes. What does that show?', '["That a ratio is a normalisation, so the same number describes 800.000000 stb of oil and 450.000000 stb of oil alike.", "That the golden was built from one row repeated at three scales, so the three are not independent evidence about the function at all.", "That the gas-oil ratio is copied through from the ledger rather than computed, which is why it does not move with the oil column.", "That the module rounds a gas-oil ratio to the nearest hundred scf/stb."]'::jsonb, 0, 'gor is gas times 1000 divided by oil, so a row that halves both terms keeps the same 500.000000000 scf/stb.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-a-ledger-and-a-question', 6, 'The published row of 2025-01-02 books 500.000000 stb of oil and 100.000000 stb of water over 12.000000 hours. It returns liquid = 600.000000000 stb and liquidPd = 1200.000000000 stb/d. What is the relation between those two?', '["liquidPd is liquid stated per producing day, so the two always stand in the ratio the hours column names and a reader can recover either from the other.", "They are the same quantity at two scales, since liquidPd is simply liquid expressed in stb/d.", "One is the volume the row booked and the other is the rate it was making it at, and nothing fixes the factor between them.", "liquid is the smoothed form of liquidPd, taken over the window the point sits in."]'::jsonb, 2, 'On the row of 2025-01-04 the same two members are 540.000000000 stb and 540.000000000 stb/d, because hours_on arrived null, so the factor is not fixed and is not visible in the pair.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-a-ledger-and-a-question', 7, 'On the published row of 2025-01-04 the oil producing-day rate is 450.000000000 stb/d and the calendar oil volume is 450.000000 stb. Why?', '["The row recorded a full 24.000000 hours, so the volume and the rate coincide by construction as they do on the row of 2025-01-01.", "hours_on arrived null, which derivePoint reads as uptime unknown, so the volume is left unscaled.", "derivePoint clamps a producing-day rate so it can never exceed the calendar volume of its own row.", "The row''s hours column held a zero, and a zero-hour row returns its volume rather than a rate."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Number.isFinite(row.hours_on) is false for a null, so the fallback is the raw volume, and nothing in the return says the column was absent rather than a full day.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-a-ledger-and-a-question', 8, 'The published row of 2025-01-05 books no oil, no water, 120.000000 Mscf of gas and 24.000000 hours. It returns watercut = null, gor = null, oilPd = 0.000000000 stb/d and gasPd = 120.000000000 Mscf/d. Which of those is an answer rather than a refusal?', '["The gas-oil ratio, because the row measured 120.000000 Mscf and a measured gas volume is enough to form one.", "The watercut, because a row that made no water has a watercut and it is zero rather than null.", "The two ratios, since a null is what this function returns when it has answered and found nothing.", "The oil producing-day rate of 0.000000000 stb/d and the gas producing-day rate of 120.000000000 Mscf/d."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The well was open for a full day and made no oil, so the rate exists and is zero; the two ratios have no denominator, so they refuse.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-a-ledger-and-a-question', 9, 'Nothing clamps hours_on to twenty-four. A constructed row of 600 stb of oil at hours_on = 26.0 returns oilPd = 553.846153846 stb/d, a ratio to the calendar volume of 0.923076923. What is wrong with that answer?', '["A producing-day rate below the calendar volume of its own row is a thing a producing-day rate cannot be.", "Nothing, since a meter reporting 26.0 hours in a day is a data problem and the arithmetic on it is correct and correctly reported as such.", "The rate should have been refused, because Number.isFinite rejects any hours value above the twenty-four the day holds.", "The ratio should have been 1.000000000, since scaling to twenty-four hours cannot change a volume booked over a longer period."]'::jsonb, 0, 'At hours_on = 168.0 the same row returns 85.714285714 stb/d at a ratio of 0.142857143, and that ratio being below one is the cheapest available check on an hours column and no function makes it.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-a-ledger-and-a-question', 10, 'A ledger books a back-out as a negative volume. What does derivePoint do with a row of 800.0 stb of oil, -900.0 stb of water and 400.0 Mscf of gas?', '["It rejects the row, because a negative volume is not a measurement.", "It does the arithmetic without complaint and returns liquid = -100.000000 stb, a null watercut because the liquid is not above zero, and an ordinary gor of 500.000000000 scf/stb.", "It returns liquid = 1700.000000 stb, reading the correction at its magnitude.", "It returns a null watercut and a null gor, both nulled by the negative liquid."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The gas-oil ratio never looks at the water at all, so a correction row is silently refused on one ratio and silently answered on the other, and the answer looks like a good day''s.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-a-ledger-and-a-question', 11, 'Two ledger rows go in and one of them carries no well. What do the two grouping functions return?', '["Both return everything, because buildWellSeries files a row with no well under a null key rather than dropping it and the two totals therefore agree.", "buildWellSeries returns 2 series, one of them unnamed, and buildFieldSeries returns 1 field day.", "buildWellSeries returns 1 series with 1 point, and buildFieldSeries returns 1 field day carrying 1800.000000 stb of oil.", "buildWellSeries raises a diagnostic naming the dropped row and returns 1 series."]'::jsonb, 2, 'buildWellSeries keys on r.well.id and silently drops any row without one, so the field total and the sum of the wells can differ by exactly those rows and nothing in either return says so.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-a-ledger-and-a-question', 12, 'Four files answer four different questions off one ledger. Which file answers which?', '["surveillance.js says which wells to go and look at, allocation.js says how many barrels a named well is booked for, and liftScreening.js says whether a rod pump suits it.", "surveillance.js books the barrels and allocation.js flags the wells that changed.", "liftAdvisor.js scores the six methods and liftScreening.js runs each design chain.", "allocation.js sizes the lift and surveillance.js reconciles it against the matrix."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Naming which function owns a number is most of the Associate tier, and the four files share nothing but the ledger row.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-a-ledger-and-a-question', 13, 'A watercut of 0.900000000000 comes off derivePoint and is passed to liftScreening.js unchanged. What has been claimed about the well?', '["That it is at 90 per cent water, which is what the row actually says.", "Nothing, since all four files carry a watercut as a fraction.", "That it is at 900 per cent water, the fraction having been scaled twice.", "That it is at 0.9 per cent water, because liftScreening.js and liftAdvisor.js carry a watercut as a per cent while derivePoint returned a 0 to 1 fraction and nothing at the boundary converts."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The same quantity has two spellings across the four files, and a row that is nine parts water in ten is described as a well that is almost dry.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-a-ledger-and-a-question', 14, 'What is a severity?', '["A measurement of how far a well has moved, scaled so that high, medium and info divide the range of possible moves into three equal bands.", "A confidence figure attached to the comparison, which is why a well with more rows in its window can reach a higher rank than one with fewer.", "The name of a threshold crossing, with rateDropPct at 20 per cent and the doubling to high at 40.", "A ranking of a well against the other wells on the field over the same window."]'::jsonb, 2, 'high, medium and info are the names of two crossings and nothing else, so a severity is not a measurement of size.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-a-ledger-and-a-question', 15, 'The published field raises 9 exceptions. What does a row''s position in that returned list tell a reader?', '["How large the move was, since within a severity the engine orders the rows by the size of the change against the baseline and prints the worst first.", "Only its severity rank and then its well name, since the sort is severity followed by String(wellName).localeCompare.", "How many rows the window mean behind it rested on, which is the closest thing in the return to a confidence ordering of the nine.", "Which of the seven exception types fired, since the list is grouped by type before it is sorted."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Severity ranks high 0, medium 1 and info 2, then the well name is compared as a string, so position carries no information about size.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-producing-day-rate', 1, 'The published row of 2025-01-01 books 800.000000 stb of oil over 24.000000 hours and the row of 2025-01-02 books 500.000000 stb over 12.000000 hours. Which row was the well performing better on?', '["The first, since it booked 800.000000 stb against 500.000000 stb and a producing-day rate is the volume the row booked.", "The second, whose oilPd is 1000.000000000 stb/d against 800.000000000 stb/d on the first.", "Neither reading is available, because the second row was open for only half a day and a rate cannot be formed from a partial day.", "They performed identically, since scaling to twenty-four hours is a presentation choice that leaves the comparison between the two rows where it was."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The second row made the smaller volume and was making it faster, and no single number off that row carries both facts.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-producing-day-rate', 2, 'A derived sweep holds one constructed row at 600 stb of oil and moves only the hours. Going from hours_on = 12.0 to hours_on = 6.0, what moves?', '["Both double, since the sweep holds the rate and lets the booked volume follow the hours column.", "The reported rate halves, because fewer hours open is less production in the day.", "Neither, because the watercut and the gas-oil ratio off that row are unchanged and they are what the sweep is about.", "The reported rate doubles from 1200.000000000 stb/d to 2400.000000000 stb/d while the oil the row booked does not move at all."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The sweep changes nothing but hours_on, so 600 stb is booked at every row of it and only the multiplier of twenty-four over the hours moves.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-producing-day-rate', 3, 'Read the uplift column of the hours sweep. Between 24.0 and 20.0 hours it moves from 1.000000000 to 1.200000000, and between 2.0 and 1.0 hours from 12.000000000 to 24.000000000. What does that shape mean for an hours column with an error in it?', '["It means the sweep is nonlinear in the wrong direction, since a rate should be least sensitive where the well was open least.", "It means an error of one hour is worth a fixed 1.000000000 of uplift wherever it falls, which is what makes the column auditable.", "The same one-hour error is worth almost nothing at the top of the range and doubles the reported rate at the bottom of it.", "It means the error is proportional to the hours, so a one-hour error at 24.0 hours costs the most."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The multiplier is twenty-four over the hours, so at 1.0 h the row reports 14400.000000000 stb/d off 600 stb and one hour either way moves it by half or double.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-producing-day-rate', 4, 'A constructed row of 600 stb of oil open for hours_on = 0.5 returns oilPd = 28800.000000000 stb/d. What is that number?', '["A correct answer to what the well would make in a day at that rate, and not a number to quote as the well''s performance.", "An arithmetic error, since a half hour of production cannot support a rate at all and the row should have refused.", "A measurement of the well''s deliverability, taken at the only half hour it was open.", "A defect in the sweep, because derivePoint refuses any hours value below 1.0."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The uplift at 0.5 h is 48.000000000, and the half hour could be the surge that follows a restart, which the arithmetic has no way to know.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-producing-day-rate', 5, 'derivePoint returns null for all four producing-day rates when hours_on is zero. Why a null rather than the Infinity the division gives?', '["Because a null is how the module marks a row it has not yet read, and the four rates are filled in later by buildWellSeries.", "Because a zero-hour row is a data error, and a null is the module''s way of rejecting the whole row.", "Because an Infinity would be printed with the wrong unit, and the module refuses rather than label a rate it cannot put stb/d after.", "An Infinity would propagate into every window mean downstream and turn a shut-in day into a fabricated record rate."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A null is skipped by a window mean where an Infinity is not, so the refusal protects every comparison built on the well.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-producing-day-rate', 6, 'The published rows of 2025-01-03 and 2025-01-05 both made no oil. What separates them?', '["Nothing in the return, since a null and a 0.000000000 stb/d rate are the same claim written twice.", "The first was never open and has no oil producing-day rate at all; the second was open a full 24.000000 hours and has one, and it is 0.000000000 stb/d.", "The first booked 120.000000 Mscf of gas and the second booked none.", "The first returns a watercut of 0.000000000000 and the second returns null."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A tool that reads a null as a zero has collapsed a well that was shut and a well that ran all day and made nothing into one story.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-producing-day-rate', 7, 'A derived sweep sets hours_on to -3.0 on a constructed row of 600 stb of oil. What comes back?', '["oilPd is null and hoursOn comes back as -3, exactly as it was handed in.", "Both come back null, because the row is rejected before either member is formed and nothing negative reaches the return.", "oilPd is negative and hoursOn is clamped to zero, which is the module''s way of marking an impossible hours value on the row.", "oilPd is 600.000000000 stb/d, because a negative hours value fails Number.isFinite and falls back to the calendar volume."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The rate is protected and the hours column is not, so a downtime check that averages hoursOn can average a negative number of hours and report it as an operating figure.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-producing-day-rate', 8, 'A ledger arrives from a spreadsheet with the hours column typed as text. On a constructed row of 600 stb of oil, the string "12" in hours_on returns what?', '["hoursOn = 12 and oilPd = 1200.000000000 stb/d, since the string coerces to a number before Number.isFinite sees it.", "A refusal on all four producing-day rates, the same as a stated zero.", "hoursOn = null and oilPd = 600.000000000 stb/d, where a numeric 12.0 on the same row gives 1200.000000000 stb/d.", "hoursOn = null and oilPd = null, because an unreadable hours column cannot support a rate."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Number.isFinite is false for a string, so the column is read as uptime unknown and the rate falls back to the volume, off by a factor of two with nothing reporting that the column was text.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-producing-day-rate', 9, 'The hours out-of-range sweep gives a ratio to the calendar volume of 0.923076923 at 26.0 hours, 0.500000000 at 48.0 hours and 0.142857143 at 168.0 hours. What single check would have caught every one of those rows?', '["That hours_on is finite, which is the test the module already makes and which every one of those rows passes on its way through.", "That the oil column is above the minOilRate of 5, which is the gate the module already carries for exactly this class of row.", "That the row''s watercut and gas-oil ratio moved when the hours did, which they would have to if the hours were wrong.", "That the ratio of the producing-day rate to the calendar volume of the same row is below one."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A producing-day rate below the calendar volume of its own row cannot happen, and no function in the module makes that comparison.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-producing-day-rate', 10, 'Why do the watercut and the gas-oil ratio off a row not move when hours_on does?', '["Both are formed from the unscaled volumes on the row, and scaling both terms of a ratio by one multiplier would not have moved them anyway.", "Because derivePoint forms both ratios after the four producing-day rates and reuses the scaled numerator with the unscaled denominator.", "Because the hours column is read only by the injector branch, which is where the scaling is applied.", "Because the module clamps hours_on to twenty-four before the two ratios are formed and after the rates are."]'::jsonb, 0, 'gasPd, waterPd and liquidPd all use the identical multiplier as oilPd, so a watercut is comparable across days when a calendar volume is not.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-producing-day-rate', 11, 'detectExceptions sets its rate key to "oil". Which column is that, and which function in surveillance.js reads the producing-day columns?', '["It is oilPd under a shorter name, and every function in the file therefore reads the producing-day rate.", "It is the calendar volume off the row, and the decline overlay is the one function in the file that reads oilPd, gasPd or liquidPd.", "It is the calendar volume, and computeKpis is the one function that reads a producing-day column instead, forming its field means off oilPd rather than off oil.", "It is the moving average of oilPd over the recent window, which buildWellSeries attaches to each point."]'::jsonb, 1, 'FIT_STREAMS pairs oil with a producing-day key oilPd and a calendar key oil, so a decline plot and an exception list off the same rows can be reading two different quantities.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-producing-day-rate', 12, 'On the teaching well OGUTA-6 the mean calendar oil falls from 502.666666666667 stb to 307.504761904762 stb while the mean producing-day oil rises from 502.666666666667 stb/d to 512.000000000000 stb/d. What did the engine report?', '["Nothing at all, because the two readings disagree and the engine holds a flag until they agree.", "A rate drop at high severity, since a fall of 38.825312618416 per cent is above the doubling point of the trigger.", "A rate drop at medium severity, printed as \"Oil down 39%: 308 vs 503 stb/d baseline.\"", "A downtime exception, since the fall is entirely in the hours column and that is the branch that reads it."]'::jsonb, 2, 'rateDropPct is 20 per cent with the doubling to high at 40, so 38.825312618416 per cent on the calendar reading raises a medium and the producing-day reading, at -1.856763925729 per cent, raises none.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-producing-day-rate', 13, 'OGUTA-6 loses 39.940476190 per cent of its mean hours against its own baseline and raises no downtime exception. Why not?', '["Its mean recent hours are 14.414285714286 h and downtimeHours is 12, so the mean is above the threshold.", "Because the downtime branch reads hoursOn only on injectors, and OGUTA-6 is typed producer.", "Because a downtime exception is suppressed once a rate_drop has already fired on the same well in the same window.", "Because the well''s baseline hours of 24.000000000000 h put it above the gate that the downtime check is measured through."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The downtime check answers one question, whether a window mean of hours crossed a fixed threshold, so a well can lose nearly forty per cent of its hours and still average above 12 h.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-producing-day-rate', 14, 'A window mean of producing-day rates is formed how in this module?', '["Weighted by each row''s hours_on, which is what makes a producing-day mean comparable across days of different uptime and is why a half-hour row cannot dominate it.", "Weighted by each row''s calendar volume, which is why the mean tracks the volume rather than the rate.", "As an unweighted arithmetic mean over the finite values in the window, so a row open for half an hour speaks as loudly as a row open all day.", "As a median over the window, which is what protects the mean from a row open for half an hour."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Each rate in the mean is an extrapolation from its own row, and nothing in the mean records how many hours stood behind each one.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-producing-day-rate', 15, 'A report says a well is down 39 per cent. What has to be stated before that sentence means anything?', '["Nothing more, because a per cent change is dimensionless and therefore carries the same meaning whichever of the two columns the two window means behind it were taken from.", "Which of the two quantities fell, since on OGUTA-6 the calendar volume fell by 38.825312618416 per cent while the producing-day rate rose by 1.856763925729 per cent.", "The severity the engine attached to it, which is what turns a change into a finding.", "The number of rows each of the two window means rested on, which the return omits."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The two readings of those same seven rows have opposite signs, so the sentence is true of one quantity and false of the other.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-ratios-a-row-carries', 1, 'What is the denominator of a watercut in derivePoint?', '["The liquid on the row, which is the oil plus the water, so a row of 500.0 stb of oil and 500.0 stb of water reads 0.500000000000.", "The oil on the row, so a row of 500.0 stb of oil and 500.0 stb of water reads 1.000000000000.", "The liquid producing-day rate, so the hours column enters the ratio.", "The field liquid on that date, which is why a well watercut moves when a neighbour shuts in."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A watercut is water over liquid, so it is bounded by 0.000000000000 and 1.000000000000 and a row of 100.0 stb of oil against 900.0 stb of water reads 0.900000000000.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-ratios-a-row-carries', 2, 'A constructed row books 100.0 stb of oil, 900.0 stb of water and 300.0 Mscf of gas, and returns a gor of 3000.000000000 scf/stb against 500.000000000 scf/stb on a row of 900.0 stb of oil, 100.0 stb of water and 450.0 Mscf. Which term moved?', '["The gas, which rose sixfold, since a gas-oil ratio can only climb when more gas is produced.", "The water, which climbed from 100.0 stb to 900.0 stb and enters the gas-oil ratio through the liquid.", "The oil, which fell by nine, while the gas fell from 450.0 Mscf to 300.0 Mscf.", "Neither: the two rows have the same liquid of 1000.000000 stb, so the ratio between them is a rounding artefact."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The gas went down and the ratio went up sixfold, because the denominator collapsed faster than the numerator did.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-ratios-a-row-carries', 3, 'A row of 400.0 stb of oil and 0.0 stb of water reads a watercut of 0.000000000000, and a row of 0.0 stb of oil and 400.0 stb of water reads 1.000000000000. What are those two values?', '["Refusals dressed as numbers, because a row missing one of the two phases cannot support a fraction and the module marks that by pinning it to an end of the range.", "Ordinary answers, since both rows made liquid and the split is simply all one way or all the other.", "Clamps, since the function bounds the ratio to the interval and both rows would otherwise have run past it.", "Defaults, applied because a zero in either column fails the finite test that guards the ratio."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A watercut of exactly one is a well making only water and it comes back as an ordinary number, and the liquid of 400.000000 stb is above zero in both cases.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-ratios-a-row-carries', 4, 'Why is a row''s watercut unchanged when its hours_on changes?', '["Because derivePoint recomputes the watercut from waterPd and liquidPd, and the one multiplier in both cancels before it is returned.", "Because the hours column is copied through as hoursOn and read only by the four producing-day rates, which the watercut is formed from.", "Because the module clamps a watercut to the value it carried on the previous row whenever the hours column moves.", "Both terms are volumes off the row and neither is scaled to twenty-four hours."]'::jsonb, 3, 'That is what makes a watercut comparable across days when a calendar volume is not, and the same is true of the gas-oil ratio.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-ratios-a-row-carries', 5, 'The published field day of 2025-06-04 carries 2100.000000 stb of oil and 770.000000 stb of water from 4 wells on stream and reports a watercut of 0.268292682927. How was that formed?', '["As the mean of the four contributing wells'' own watercuts, which is what a field figure means.", "As the watercut of the largest contributing well, the others being folded in as a weighting.", "Volumetrically, as the water sum over the liquid sum of 2870.000000 stb.", "As a moving average of the field watercut over the trailing window that ends on that date."]'::jsonb, 2, 'buildFieldSeries adds every row on a date and then divides once, so a large well moves the field figure and a small one barely does.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-ratios-a-row-carries', 6, 'Where does a gas-oil ratio refuse, and where does a watercut refuse?', '["The gas-oil ratio is null when the row''s oil is not above zero, and the watercut is null when the row''s liquid is not above zero.", "Both are null when the row booked no gas, which is the one column they share.", "The gas-oil ratio refuses on a null hours column and the watercut refuses on a zero one.", "Both refuse on the same condition, an empty row."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A constructed row of 0.0 stb of oil, 400.0 stb of water and 200.0 Mscf of gas therefore carries a watercut of 1.000000000000 and a gor of null however much gas it booked.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-ratios-a-row-carries', 7, 'A well waters out steadily while making the same gas per barrel of oil. What do its two row ratios do?', '["Both climb together, since the gas-oil ratio takes its denominator from the liquid and the liquid is rising as the water does.", "The watercut climbs and the gas-oil ratio stays flat, because water appears nowhere in the gas-oil ratio.", "The watercut climbs and the gas-oil ratio falls, the extra water displacing gas out of the ratio.", "Both stay flat, since the two ratios are normalised against one another before the point is returned and the module reports only the pair."]'::jsonb, 1, 'gor is gas times 1000 divided by oil, and the 1000 is scf per Mscf and the only unit conversion in the file.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-ratios-a-row-carries', 8, 'A correction row books positive oil against negative water. What does derivePoint hand back that a reader could mistake for a good day?', '["It refuses both ratios, so the correction is invisible to anything reading only the ratio columns.", "It carries a watercut of -1.000000000000, which no chart axis will show.", "It carries a gor of 500.000000000 scf/stb, an ordinary figure that looks like a good day''s, beside a liquid of -100.000000 stb.", "It raises a diagnostic that the studio does not display."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The watercut is nulled because the liquid is not above zero, and the gas-oil ratio never looks at the water at all, so one ratio is silently refused and the other is silently answered.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-ratios-a-row-carries', 9, 'A constructed row of -500.0 stb of oil, 200.0 stb of water and 400.0 Mscf of gas comes back with liquid = -300.000000 stb, watercut = null and gor = null. Why is the gas-oil ratio null here when it was 500.000000000 scf/stb on the other correction row?', '["Because the liquid is negative on this row and negative on the other, and the gas-oil ratio inherits the liquid''s refusal on both of them in the same way.", "Because the gas column is larger than the oil column here, and the ratio refuses above a ceiling that the other row sat below.", "Because the watercut refused first, and every later member of the point is nulled once one member has refused.", "Because this row''s oil is not above zero, which is the only condition that nulls a gas-oil ratio."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The other correction row carried 800.0 stb of positive oil, so it had a denominator; a negative oil volume does not.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-ratios-a-row-carries', 10, 'A chart fills a null watercut with a zero before plotting. What has it stated?', '["That a row which made no liquid at all made liquid and none of it was water, which is what the published row of 2025-01-05 and a constructed row of 400.0 stb of oil and 0.0 stb of water respectively say.", "Nothing, since a null watercut and a zero watercut are two spellings of the same absence.", "That the row was shut in, which is the claim a zero hours column would make.", "That the row''s liquid was negative, since that is the only case a null arises from."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A null watercut means the row made no liquid and a zero watercut means it made liquid and none of it was water, and a chart that draws them at the same height has stated something the engine refused to state.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-ratios-a-row-carries', 11, 'detectExceptions forms a window mean over the finite values in the window. What does a null ratio row do to that mean?', '["It pulls the mean towards zero, which is why a well with shut-in days reads a lower watercut than it carried.", "It is skipped rather than counted as a zero, so the mean can rest on fewer rows than the window holds.", "It voids the whole window, and the comparison is not made for that well.", "It is carried forward from the last finite row, so the mean is over the full row count either way."]'::jsonb, 1, 'On the teaching well OGUTA-2 the recent watercut read that way is 0.451386451920 over 7 rows and the baseline is 0.236641221374 over 30 rows, and how many rows a mean rested on is not printed beside it anywhere a studio user sees.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-ratios-a-row-carries', 12, 'The teaching well OGUTA-2 has a recent window of 7 rows carrying 4327.000000000 stb of oil, 1964.000000000 stb of water and 2792.000000000 Mscf of gas. Read two ways, what is its gas-oil ratio?', '["1066.663410762250 scf/stb either way, since the two readings are algebraically the same quantity.", "645.250751097758 scf/stb, which is the only defensible reading of a period and the one both shipped functions take.", "1066.663410762250 scf/stb read as a mean of daily ratios, and 645.250751097758 scf/stb read volumetrically.", "It cannot be formed, because three of the seven rows collapsed and a window that mixes rates has no period ratio."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The first reading is 1.653099061020 times the second, and neither is wrong: they answer two different questions off identical rows.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-ratios-a-row-carries', 13, 'Over the OGUTA-2 baseline window both readings of the gas-oil ratio give 580.000000000000 scf/stb, a difference of -0.000000000000. What makes them agree there?', '["The baseline window holds 30 rows against 7 in the recent window, and a mean over enough rows converges on the volumetric reading whatever the rows are.", "The baseline oil of 31167.000000000 stb is large enough that the weighting a volumetric reading applies cannot change the answer at that size.", "The two readings were reconciled before the baseline was stored, which is why only recent windows can show a disagreement.", "The daily ratio does not move at all across that window, and where the daily ratio is constant the two readings collapse to one number."]'::jsonb, 3, 'That is why a window of uniform days cannot show the problem at all, and the disagreement appears only where the window mixes rates.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-ratios-a-row-carries', 14, 'The teaching well OGUTA-2 goes from an ordinary recent day of 1008.000000 stb of oil, 312.000000 stb of water and 585.000000 Mscf of gas to a collapsed one of 82.000000 stb, 231.000000 stb and 141.000000 Mscf. What does the gas-oil ratio rise of 2.962851782364 establish about the gas?', '["Nothing, since the oil fell by a factor of 12.292682926829 while the gas fell by a factor of 4.148936170213, so the ratio rose on a falling numerator.", "That gas broke out of solution below the bubble point, which is the mechanism a rising gas-oil ratio names and the reason the ratio is watched at all on a producing well.", "That the gas rate roughly tripled, which is what a ratio rise of that size measures.", "That the gas cap advanced, since the water rose by only 1.350649350649 over the same pair."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A ratio cannot tell you which of its two terms did it, and on that pair the quantity that moved most was the oil.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-ratios-a-row-carries', 15, 'Which of the three shipped functions forms a period watercut as an unweighted mean of daily ratios?', '["computeKpis, which takes the mean of the field water and the mean of the field oil and then forms the ratio, weighting every day by what it produced.", "detectExceptions, which calls windowMean on the watercut key of each point.", "buildFieldSeries, which sums the columns per day and divides once.", "derivePoint, which forms a watercut per row and hands the mean up."]'::jsonb, 1, 'computeKpis and buildFieldSeries are both volumetric, off means and off sums respectively, and all three are in the shipped studio.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-from-rows-to-a-field', 1, 'The published surveillance case is 268 ledger rows. Grouped one way it is 51 field days and grouped the other it is 7 series. What does each function key on?', '["buildFieldSeries keys on the date and returns one point per field day, and buildWellSeries keys on r.well.id and returns one series per well.", "buildFieldSeries keys on the well and buildWellSeries keys on the date.", "Both key on the date, and buildWellSeries then splits each field day by well, which is why the two objects always reconcile to the same 268 rows on the published case.", "Both key on the well, and buildFieldSeries then sums the series it was given."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Neither asks the other anything and neither is a filtered version of the other, so the same 268 rows become two objects with nothing in common but the rows.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-from-rows-to-a-field', 2, 'P-3 files 6 points on the published ledger and P-4 files 27, where P-1 files 47. Which of the two is the well with a gap in it?', '["P-3, since 6 points against 47 is the larger shortfall and a shortfall is what a gap is.", "Both, since any series below the 47 points the field''s daily wells file has lost rows by definition and the count is the measure of it.", "P-4, which files at a cadence of 1.000000 days and is missing some of those days.", "Neither, because seriesCadenceDays reports 1.000000 for both of them and a gap would move that figure."]'::jsonb, 2, 'P-3 reports at a cadence of 30.000000 days, so 6 points is its full record, and a point count is not a data quality score.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-from-rows-to-a-field', 3, 'Two ledger rows go in and one carries no well. What can differ between the field oil and the sum of the well series, and what says so?', '["Nothing, since buildWellSeries files an unattached row under a null key and both objects therefore see all 268 rows on the published case.", "Exactly the rows nobody attached a well to, and nothing in either return says so.", "The rows whose hours column was blank, which buildWellSeries reads as uptime unknown and drops on that ground before the series is formed.", "Any amount at all, since the two functions read different columns of the row and a reconciliation figure is returned beside each."]'::jsonb, 1, 'buildWellSeries silently drops a row with no well: hand it two rows of which one has none and it returns 1 series with 1 point, while buildFieldSeries returns 1 field day carrying 1800.000000 stb of oil.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-from-rows-to-a-field', 4, 'The published field day of 2025-06-30 carries 9740.000000 stb of oil, 2910.000000 stb of water and 6167.000000 Mscf of gas, and reports a watercut of 0.230039525692 and a gor of 633.162217659 scf/stb. In what order did that happen?', '["The ratios were formed on each row and then averaged, which is why the field figure sits between the wells'' own.", "The ratios were carried over from the previous field day, 2025-06-29, and rescaled by the change in the oil sum.", "The oil and water sums were formed off the ratios, which the ledger carries per row and the function accumulates.", "The four columns were summed first and the two ratios were then formed off the sums."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Liquid is the oil sum plus the water sum, the watercut is the water sum over that liquid, and the gas-oil ratio is the gas sum times 1000 over the oil sum.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-from-rows-to-a-field', 5, 'Four identical ledger rows of 800 stb of oil on one date, with the volume column typed as text, give a field oil of 800800800800.000000000 stb where the same rows as numbers give 3200.000000000 stb. What did that?', '["The accumulator started at a numeric zero and the first string turned it into a string, so every later row was concatenated onto it rather than added.", "A unit error: the text rows were read in a different volume unit, and the field total therefore carries four rows converted at a factor the return does not name anywhere.", "A duplication, the same row having been counted once per character in it.", "A rounding failure, since a string cannot carry the precision a stb column needs."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Two such rows already give 800800.000000000 stb, an overstatement factor of 500.500000000000, and nothing in the return says a column arrived as text.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-from-rows-to-a-field', 6, 'One ledger row of 800 stb of oil and 200 stb of water, typed as text, is put through buildFieldSeries alone. Which members are right and which are wrong?', '["All four are wrong, since a text column corrupts every member formed from it.", "All four are right, since the accumulator coerces a string before it is added.", "The oil of 800.000000000 stb and the water of 200.000000000 stb are right, and the liquid of 8000200.000000000 stb and the watercut of 0.000024999375 are wrong.", "The liquid is right and the watercut is wrong, the watercut being the only division."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The two sums each ran once and came back as themselves, and the damage is entirely in the member formed by adding one string to another.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-from-rows-to-a-field', 7, 'What are the two clauses behind the wellsOn count on a field day?', '["That the row carries a well of type producer, and that its oil column is above the minOilRate of 5, which is the gate every producing count in the domain is taken through.", "That oil_stb plus water_stb plus gas_mscf is above zero, and that hours_on is either null or above zero.", "That the row''s liquid is above zero, and that its watercut is a finite number rather than one of the nulls derivePoint can return.", "That the well appears on the previous field day too, and that its injection column is zero."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Nothing else is consulted: not the well type, not the injection column, and the first clause adds stb to Mscf to make one boolean.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-from-rows-to-a-field', 8, 'A producer books 900.000000 stb of oil on a date with hours_on recorded as zero. What does the field day for that date carry?', '["A wellsOn of 1 and a field oil of 900.000000 stb, since the volume clause is met and it is the clause the count is named for.", "A wellsOn of 0 and a field oil of 0.000000 stb, the zero hours having excluded the row from the sums as well as from the count.", "A wellsOn of 1 and a field oil of 0.000000 stb, since a zero-hours row counts as present and contributes nothing.", "A wellsOn of 0 and a field oil of 900.000000 stb, both in the same returned object."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Zero is not above zero, so the second clause rejects the row from the count while its oil goes into the total unchanged, and the volume and the count disagree by construction.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-from-rows-to-a-field', 9, 'An injector takes 3000 stb of water on a field day. What does it do to that day''s counts and totals?', '["It contributes a winj of 3000.000000 stb and a wellsOn of 0, because injection is not one of the three columns the first clause adds.", "A wellsOn of 1 and a winj of 3000.000000 stb, since a row that moved fluid was on stream.", "Nothing at all, since buildFieldSeries filters injector rows out before summing.", "A wellsOn of 1 and no injection, the well type having been read off the row."]'::jsonb, 0, 'buildFieldSeries filters on nothing and never asks what type a well is, so injector and observation rows land in the totals and add nothing to oil, water or gas.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-from-rows-to-a-field', 10, 'On the published field, 2025-06-30 carries a wellsOn of 3 while computeKpis on the same ledger reports a wellCount of 7 and a producerCount of 6. What is the relation between the three?', '["The first is the subset of the second that produced, and the third sits between them.", "The first counts rows, the second counts wells and the third counts producers, so 3 plus 6 is the 9 exceptions the field raises.", "Three counts with three definitions, and none of them is a count of wells that made oil.", "They would agree on a complete ledger, and the spread between them measures how many rows the field failed to file that day."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A gas-only row and a water-only row each count as one well on stream while contributing 0.000000 stb of oil, and 2025-06-28 on the same ledger carries a wellsOn of 2.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-from-rows-to-a-field', 11, 'A series has gaps of 1, 1, 30 and 30 days. seriesCadenceDays returns 15.500000. What is odd about that figure?', '["Nothing, since 15.500000 is the arithmetic mean of the four gaps and a cadence is a mean of gaps.", "No gap in that series was ever fifteen and a half days, because a median over an even count averages the two middle gaps.", "It should have been 30.000000, since the widening rule rounds a cadence up to the coarser of the two reporting habits it found.", "It should have been 1.000000, since the median of an even count takes the lower of the two middle values."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The cadence is the median gap, and it is the number every widening rule in detectExceptions is built on.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-from-rows-to-a-field', 12, 'What does seriesCadenceDays return for a series of one point?', '["null, because one point has no gaps and the function refuses rather than guess a cadence from nothing.", "1.000000, the default cadence a series is given until a second point arrives, since every widening rule downstream needs a finite number to multiply.", "0.000000, since the total span of a one-point series is zero days.", "The field cadence, taken from the other series handed in on the same call, which is how a new well inherits a reporting habit before it has one of its own."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A single gap of 1 day returns 1.000000 and gaps of 7, 7 and 7 return 7.000000, so the refusal is specific to having no gap at all.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-from-rows-to-a-field', 13, 'The teaching well OGUTA-14 files six period rows. Averaged over windowDays of 7 the output is 26400, 25600, 24800, 24000, 23200 and 11600 stb, which is the input unchanged. Why?', '["The moving average keeps the first point of each window and discards the rest, so any window returns its own first value.", "The function refuses to smooth a series whose cadence is coarser than the window and returns the input as a documented fallback.", "The window is a count of points rather than a span of days, and a count of 7 exceeds the six points the series holds.", "The window is a date window, and at a cadence of 30.000000 days every 7-day window holds exactly one point and averages it."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The final point of that series reads 11600 stb at 7 days and 22600 stb at 200 days, and nothing on the chart says the smoothing did nothing.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-from-rows-to-a-field', 14, 'decimate is asked for at most 1500 points. Off a 1501-point series it returns 751 and off a 3000-point series it returns 1501. Why?', '["The function rescales the stride to fill the budget, and the two cases sit either side of the rescaling threshold.", "The 1501-point series was decimated twice, once on the way in and once on the way out.", "The stride is Math.ceil(n / maxPoints), an integer, so one extra point takes the stride from 1 to 2 and the always-keep-the-last rule puts the other case over the cap.", "maxPoints is a floor rather than a cap on the returned count, so 751 is the anomaly and 1501 is correct."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The published case is n = 3200 at maxPoints = 1500, which strides by 3 and returns 1068 points with a last index kept of 3199.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-from-rows-to-a-field', 15, 'What does decimate report about the points it dropped?', '["A dropped count on the returned array, which is what lets a caller size a download from maxPoints with the returned length known in advance.", "Nothing at all: it raises no diagnostic, carries no threshold and returns no count of what was removed.", "A flag on each surviving point naming the stride it was kept by.", "A warning whenever the returned length exceeds the cap the argument named."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A dropped point is a day, so a peak that fell on a skipped index is simply not in the output, and the series that comes back is a valid series of real points and is not the series that went in.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-field-kpis', 1, 'What makes computeKpis the one surveillance function that belongs in a tier with no comparison in it?', '["It reads a single ledger row, which is the smallest input any function in the file takes and the reason it cannot compare anything.", "It has no baseline, no second window and no verdict, so it can only say what the field averaged.", "It compares the trailing window against the window before it and returns only the later of the two, which is why no baseline appears in the return.", "It returns integers, and a count cannot carry a comparison the way a rate can."]'::jsonb, 1, 'It takes the field series, keeps the last windowDays of it by date, and averages, and there is nothing in the return to compare that against.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-field-kpis', 2, 'On the published field, computeKpis at windowDays of 60 averages 47 field days and at 180 and at 365 averages the same 51, returning an oil of 3256.666666667 stb/d for both. What does the object report as windowDays?', '["51 in both cases, the count of days it actually averaged, which is what makes the two returns distinguishable.", "47 in both cases, the last count at which the ledger was complete.", "The number of field days it averaged, rounded up to the nearest whole window.", "180 in the one case and 365 in the other, exactly as asked, since windowDays comes back unchanged."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The window is a date window over the field series, so a ledger with holes averages fewer days than asked and nothing in the return distinguishes a field that reported every day from one with a fortnight missing.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-field-kpis', 3, 'The published 7-day return reports an oil of 1941.428571429 stb/d. That figure is a mean of what?', '["The field days in the window, each entering at that day''s total calendar oil volume, with no hours column consulted anywhere in the averaging.", "The producing-day oil rates of the wells in the window, averaged first per well and then across the field.", "The oilPd column on every point in the window, which is what the stb/d on the label refers to.", "The wells'' own mean rates, weighted by each well''s uptime over the window."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The return labels it stb/d, and a day on which every well ran four hours enters the mean at its calendar volume like any other.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-field-kpis', 4, 'computeKpis forms its watercut and gas-oil ratio in what order?', '["The ratio off each field day first, then the mean of those ratios, which is what windowMean does on the same field.", "The ratio off each well first, then the mean weighted by the wells that reported inside the window.", "Mean of the field oil, mean of the field water and mean of the field gas first, then the two ratios off those means.", "The ratio off the first and last field day in the window, the interior days entering only through the means."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Forming the ratios off means makes them volume weighted by construction, and at 7 days on the published field they are 0.337719298246 and 749.963208241 scf/stb.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-field-kpis', 5, 'detectExceptions reads the gor and watercut members of each point through windowMean. What quantity does that give?', '["The same volumetric ratio computeKpis returns, arrived at by a different route through the same points.", "An unweighted arithmetic mean of daily ratios, in which a day that made almost nothing speaks as loudly as a day that made everything.", "A ratio of the summed volumes over the window, since each point ratio carries its own volumes with it.", "A median of the daily ratios, which is what protects the comparison from a low-rate day."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Each of those ratios was formed in derivePoint from one row alone, so the mean is over ratios and never over volumes.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-field-kpis', 6, 'On the published well P-1 the recent gas-oil ratio reads 1360.267857142857 scf/stb one way and 1141.902313624679 scf/stb the other, against a baseline of 800.000000000000 scf/stb both ways. What does the choice between the two readings decide?', '["Nothing that reaches a reader, because the engine reports both readings side by side and lets the studio pick.", "Whether the exception fires at all, since one of the two rises is below the 30 per cent trigger and the other is above it.", "Only the size of the printed figure, the severity being fixed by the baseline of 800.000000000000 scf/stb which both readings share.", "Whether the printed severity is high or medium, since the rises are 70.033482142857 per cent and 42.737789203085 per cent against a gorRisePct of 30 with the doubling to high at 60."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The overstatement of one reading over the other is 19.122961825433 per cent, and the golden publishes it as a measured disagreement rather than resolving it.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-field-kpis', 7, 'Both readings put the P-1 baseline watercut at 0.352517985612 and then split on the recent window, 0.561904761905 against 0.538552787663. Why do they agree on the baseline?', '["Because the daily ratios over that window do not move, and where a daily ratio is constant a mean of ratios and a ratio of sums are the same number.", "Because a baseline of 30 days holds enough rows for the two readings to converge on one another, where a recent window of only 7 days does not have the length to do it.", "Because the baseline is stored once and reused by both readings rather than recomputed.", "Because a watercut is bounded by one, and the bound binds harder on the longer window."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The rises are 20.938677629325 points read one way and 18.603480205160 points read the other, and the disagreement lives entirely in the recent window.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-field-kpis', 8, 'How does computeKpis decide which points contribute to uptimePct?', '["It reads the hours_on on every point of every series handed in, injectors included, and divides the total by twenty-four times the point count.", "It reads only the wells whose type is producer, which is the same set producerCount reports.", "It walks the well series, skips anything typed injector, and counts one slot for every remaining point that carries a finite hours_on.", "It reads the wellsOn count off each field day and averages that against the wellCount."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A point with no hours recorded is not a zero, it is not a slot at all, so a producer that never fills the column in is silently absent from the figure.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-field-kpis', 9, 'The published uptime is 44.444444444 per cent at windowDays of 7 and 88.333333333 per cent at 30, with 72.222222222 per cent at 14 and 93.055555556 per cent at 60. What changed between them?', '["The field''s operating performance, which improved steadily as the ledger ran on and the figure records that improvement.", "The membership, since a longer window brings more wells into the uptime and each of them contributes its own slots to the mean.", "The set of wells typed injector, which the uptime skips and which a wider window redefines.", "Only the window, since all four are the same function on the same field and the same ledger."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The uptime is the hours summed over the slots as a fraction of twenty-four, so widening the window changes which slots are in it and nothing about the field.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-field-kpis', 10, 'The teaching well OGUTA-21 is typed observation, books every volume as zero and records 24 hours on stream on all 70 of its days. What does it do to the field uptime?', '["Nothing, since computeKpis skips a well that produced nothing before counting slots.", "It raises it, from 90.014880952 per cent to 92.011904762 per cent at windowDays of 7, a difference of 1.997023810 points.", "It lowers it, since a well that made no oil is read as downtime on every one of its days.", "Nothing at windowDays of 7 and 0.465972222 points at 30, the observation well entering the mean only once the window is wide enough to reach its earlier rows."]'::jsonb, 1, 'computeKpis skips only injectors from the uptime and reads everything else, so a field could raise its reported uptime by adding a well that never produces, provided that well fills in its hours.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-field-kpis', 11, 'Of the 7 series read for uptime on the teaching field, only 5 carry an hours_on on any row. What are the other two?', '["Wells that record no hours at all, so they contribute no slots and are absent from the uptime whatever they produced.", "Injectors, which the uptime skips before the slot count begins, so they are the two series present in wellCount and missing from the uptime membership.", "Wells that were shut for the whole window and entered the mean at zero hours, which is what pulls the figure below the wells that actually ran.", "Wells outside the window, dropped by the date filter before any slot is counted, so their hours were never available to the mean at all."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The uptime is the mean of the wells that happened to fill the column in, and there is no slot count, no well list and no membership figure anywhere in the returned object.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-field-kpis', 12, 'The published field hands in 7 series, of which 1 is typed injector, 1 is typed observation and 5 are typed producer. Why is producerCount 6?', '["Because it counts a series as a producer once it has booked oil on any day inside the window, and the observation well booked oil on one.", "Because it counts the wells with a cadence finer than the widening threshold, which the observation well meets and the injector does not.", "Because it excludes one type only, injector, and an observation well is not an injector.", "Because wellCount is 7 and producerCount is defined as one less than it."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Exclusion of one type is not selection of another, so the field has five producers and the object says six.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-field-kpis', 13, 'On the teaching field, wellCount reads 8 and producerCount reads 7 at windowDays of 7, 14, 30 and 70 alike. Why do neither move?', '["Because the field''s eight wells all report on every day of every one of those windows, so no widening can add or remove one.", "Both are taken off the series list before any windowing happens.", "Because the counts are recomputed per window and the four windows happen to select the same wells.", "Because a count is an integer and the function rounds it to the nearest whole well."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A well that filed one row in September and nothing since counts exactly as much as a well that filed every day, and wellCount is simply the length of the series list handed in.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-field-kpis', 14, 'A field series comes back with a null oil mean and a water mean of 5 stb. What does computeKpis return for liquid and for watercut?', '["Both null, since the explicit null check that guards the liquid guards the watercut on the next line as well.", "liquid = 5.000000 and a watercut of 1.000000000000, the null oil mean reading as a zero in the sum.", "Both refused, since computeKpis returns null outright when any mean in it is null.", "liquid = null and watercut = 1.000000000000, one line apart and off the same pair of means."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Liquid carries an explicit null check on both means and the watercut on the very next line reads the same two means with a bare test that oil plus water is above zero.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-field-kpis', 15, 'computeKpis is handed an empty field series. What comes back?', '["An object with every rate at 0.000000 and both counts at zero, which a caller cannot distinguish from a quiet field.", "An object with every member null except windowDays and the two counts, which come back as asked and as zero respectively.", "null outright, rather than an object of zeroes.", "The previous return, cached against the same asOf."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A field day of all zeroes is the other case and it does return an object, carrying a liquid of 0.000000, a null watercut, a null gor and a null uptimePct.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 1, 'A graded field asks for a figure about a period on the whole field. Which of the two functions in reach owns it?', '["derivePoint, since every field figure is assembled out of the fourteen keys it returns on each row.", "detectExceptions, which is the function that reads a window and is therefore the one a period question belongs to.", "computeKpis, which sees a trailing window of field days and returns means, the two ratios of those means, an uptime and two counts.", "buildWellSeries, which groups the rows the period covers before any figure is formed."]'::jsonb, 2, 'derivePoint sees one ledger row and nothing else, so a question about one row is the first and a question about a period on the whole field is the second, and neither takes a baseline.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 2, 'academy_submit_capstone compares an answer against an expected value how?', '["As an absolute difference in that field''s own units against a tolerance in the same units, which is not a percentage of anything.", "As a relative error, so a watercut carried as a per cent and the same watercut as a fraction are near misses of each other.", "As a match to a stated number of significant figures, which is why the digest prints twelve of them.", "As a match after both values are normalised to SI units."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A watercut carried as a fraction and a watercut carried as a per cent are two different quantities under that rule, not two roundings of one.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 3, 'Before writing any producing-day rate down, what is the first thing to check on the row?', '["The oil column, since a rate is refused whenever the oil is below the minOilRate of 5 that gates the module.", "The date, since a row read as a calendar day when it covers a month gives a rate off by the length of the period.", "The well type, since the four producing-day rates are formed only on rows carrying a producer.", "The hours column, since a null there is read as uptime unknown and the rate falls back to the unscaled volume."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The published row of 2025-01-04 returns an oilPd of 450.000000000 stb/d against a calendar volume of 450.000000 stb, and the two are the same number and not the same claim.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 4, 'The published rows of 2025-01-03 and 2025-01-05 return a null oil producing-day rate and a rate of 0.000000000 stb/d respectively. Which of the two is a refusal?', '["The zero, since a rate of zero is the value the function returns when it declines to answer and a null is a missing input.", "The null, because the well was never open and dividing by zero hours would have given an Infinity.", "Both, since neither row booked any oil and a rate needs a numerator.", "Neither, since both rows returned a value and a refusal has to raise a diagnostic."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The second row was open a full 24.000000 hours and made no oil, so the rate exists and is zero, and only one of the two answers is a refusal.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 5, 'Which five members of a derived point can be null, and what nulls each?', '["liquid never refuses; watercut nulls on no liquid, gor on no oil, and the four producing-day rates on zero hours.", "watercut on no oil, gor on no gas, and the four producing-day rates on a null hours column.", "The four producing-day rates on a null hours column, and liquid on a negative volume.", "All five null together whenever any column on the row is missing."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The five computed members are liquid, watercut, gor and the four producing-day rates counted as one family, and each carries its own condition.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 6, 'A question asks for a period gas-oil ratio. What has to be settled before the number can be written?', '["The severity band it falls in, since the reading follows from the band the engine printed it under.", "Whether the well was on a daily or a monthly cadence, since the two readings coincide on a daily ledger.", "Which of the two readings is wanted, since on the published well P-1 the rise is 70.033482142857 per cent one way and 42.737789203085 per cent the other.", "The number of significant figures, which is the only difference between the two figures a studio can print."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A ratio written without its reading is not an answer to anything, and computeKpis and buildFieldSeries form theirs volumetrically while detectExceptions takes an unweighted mean of daily ratios.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 7, 'A question asks for a field figure and the answer is written off buildWellSeries instead. What can go wrong?', '["Nothing, since the field series is the sum of the well series by construction and either route reaches the same total.", "The two can differ by exactly the rows nobody attached a well to, since buildWellSeries keys on r.well.id and drops any row carrying none.", "The well route double counts, since a row carrying a well is read once by each of the two grouping functions and once again by the field roll-up that follows.", "The well route is refused, since buildWellSeries cannot be called without a field series to key against."]'::jsonb, 1, 'There is no reconciliation figure anywhere in either object, so nothing says a row went missing.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 8, 'A volume column arrives as text and the roll-up concatenates instead of adding. What is the general lesson for a reader of any aggregate?', '["That a volume column must be validated before it is summed, which is the check buildFieldSeries makes and reports on the returned field day.", "That the accumulator should have started at null rather than at a numeric zero, which is the fix and the reason the case is worth printing.", "That a text column is a data-loading problem rather than an engine one, since nothing inside the function could have seen it.", "That aggregation is a choice, and the choice shows when it goes wrong."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The accumulator started at a numeric zero and the first string turned it into a string, so every later row was concatenated onto it, and nothing in the return says a column arrived as text.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 9, 'What is the mistake this whole tier is aimed at?', '["Treating a returned number as a fact about a well, when it is a reading of a period made by a named function under a stated convention.", "Quoting too few digits, since the numbers a studio prints are rounded to whole barrels and the graded ones are committed to twelve decimal places instead.", "Trusting the golden over the shipped engine, when the golden and the engine are reproduced against each other on every one of the 51 published field days.", "Reading a null as a missing value rather than as a zero, which collapses a row that was shut in and a row that ran all day and made nothing into one story."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The number alone does not carry which function, which window or which convention produced it.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 10, 'Name a sentence that nothing in this tier can support.', '["That the field averaged an oil of 1941.428571429 stb/d over a trailing 7 days anchored on its own latest ledger date.", "That a row''s watercut is a fraction of the liquid the row made.", "That a well changed, since neither derivePoint nor computeKpis has a second window to compare against.", "That a field day''s two ratios were formed volumetrically, off the summed columns of every row carrying that date."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Both functions return numbers and neither returns a verdict, and a reader who wants a well worth looking at has to get it from detectExceptions.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 11, 'What are the eight default surveillance settings?', '["recentDays 7 days, baselineDays 30 days, rateDropPct 20 per cent, watercutRisePts 10 points, gorRisePct 30 per cent, downtimeHours 12 h, staleDays 7 days and maxTestAgeDays 180 days.", "recentDays 7 days, baselineDays 30 days, rateDropPct 20 per cent, watercutRisePts 10 points, gorRisePct 30 per cent, downtimeHours 12 h, staleDays 7 days and minOilRate 5.", "recentDays 7 days, baselineDays 30 days, rateDropPct 40 per cent, watercutRisePts 20 points, gorRisePct 60 per cent and downtimeHours 12 h.", "windowDays 7 days, factorWarnLow 0.7, factorWarnHigh 1.3, minDurationHours 4 h, outlierPct 50 per cent and tolerancePct 35 per cent."]'::jsonb, 1, 'maxTestAgeDays of 180 and the factor band of 0.7 to 1.3 belong to the allocation defaults, and the doubling points are not settings of their own.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 12, 'At a series cadence of 30.0 days, what does the widening rule do to the three window lengths?', '["All three are held at their defaults of 7, 30 and 7, since the widening rule applies to the field and not to a series.", "recentDays becomes 30, baselineDays becomes 30 and staleDays becomes 30, each matched to the cadence itself.", "recentDays becomes 45 and baselineDays becomes 120, with staleDays held at its default of 7 days so that a coarse well is still called stale on time.", "recentDays becomes 45, baselineDays becomes 120 and staleDays becomes 45."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Each well widens on its own cadence, so two wells on one field can be compared over windows of very different lengths with nothing in the return saying so.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 13, 'Why does the golden publish the P-1 ratio seam as a measured disagreement rather than resolving it?', '["Because resolving it would move numbers a shipped studio shows, and the overstatement of one reading over the other is 19.122961825433 per cent.", "Because neither reading has yet been checked against an independent oracle, so the golden records the pair as a placeholder until one of the two can be confirmed.", "Because the two readings sit within the absolute tolerance the grader applies to that field, so there is no disagreement large enough to be worth resolving at all.", "Because the disagreement is a rounding artefact of the twelve digits the golden commits."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A surveillance tool needs a mean of daily ratios to notice a well that has changed and a volumetric ratio to book a barrel, and both are shipped.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 14, 'What units does this tier require on the four quantities a capstone most often asks for?', '["A ledger volume in stb/d, a producing-day rate in stb over a row, a gas-oil ratio in Mscf/stb and a watercut carried as a per cent throughout the four files.", "All four in the units the studio prints, since the grader normalises before it compares.", "A ledger volume in stb over a row, a producing-day rate in stb/d, a gas-oil ratio in scf/stb and a surveillance watercut as a fraction.", "A ledger volume in stb, a producing-day rate in stb/d, a gas-oil ratio in scf/stb and a watercut as a per cent, which is the spelling the two lift files use."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Gas is in Mscf over a row, hours in h, a window in days and an uptime as a per cent, and a watercut is a per cent only in the two lift modules.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 15, 'What are the two questions this tier is explicitly unable to answer?', '["How much a well made over one ledger row, and what fraction of the liquid on that row was water rather than oil, both of which derivePoint returns.", "Who made a metered barrel, which is a different module, and which lift method a well should carry, which is two more.", "What a field averaged over a trailing window of days, and how many well series were handed in to the call that averaged them all.", "What a row''s gas-oil ratio is, and whether the hours column was filled in."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Everything in this tier is one pass over rows, and the comparison half begins with detectExceptions putting two windows on one well.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'final', null, 1, 'A studio draws a decline plot and an exception list from one ledger in one session. Which column does each of them read?', '["The decline overlay reads the producing-day columns oilPd, gasPd and liquidPd, and detectExceptions reads the calendar volume under the key oil.", "Both read oilPd, since a decline and a change are both statements about rate.", "Both read oil, since the producing-day columns are formed only on request.", "The decline overlay reads oil and detectExceptions reads oilPd."]'::jsonb, 0, 'FIT_STREAMS pairs each phase with a producing-day key and a calendar key, and the decline overlay is the one function in surveillance.js that reads the producing-day side.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'final', null, 2, 'Two figures on one screen are both labelled stb/d: a computeKpis oil of 1941.428571429 and a derivePoint oilPd. Which of the two consulted an hours column?', '["Only the KPI figure, since its window is where the uptime is formed and the two share it.", "Both, since a rate carrying stb/d has to have been scaled to twenty-four hours to earn the label.", "Only the producing-day rate, since no hours column is consulted anywhere in the KPI averaging.", "Neither, since the hours column reaches a rate only through the exception comparison."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A day on which every well ran four hours enters the KPI mean at its calendar volume like any other, so the label is the same and the quantity is not.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'final', null, 3, 'Three shipped functions form a period watercut off the same rows. Which one is not volumetric?', '["buildFieldSeries, which averages the watercut each contributing well reported on the date.", "detectExceptions, which takes an unweighted mean of the daily ratios through windowMean.", "computeKpis, which takes the median of the field days in its window.", "derivePoint, which forms a watercut per row and then weights it by the hours."]'::jsonb, 1, 'computeKpis forms the ratio off the means of the field oil, water and gas, and buildFieldSeries forms it off the summed columns of one date, so both are volume weighted by construction.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'final', null, 4, 'A producer books 900.000000 stb of oil on a date and records hours_on as zero. What do derivePoint and buildFieldSeries each do with that row?', '["Both refuse the row, so neither the rate nor the volume reaches anything downstream.", "derivePoint returns an oilPd of 900.000000000 stb/d and buildFieldSeries counts a wellsOn of 1.", "derivePoint nulls the rates and buildFieldSeries drops the volume as well as the count.", "derivePoint nulls all four producing-day rates and buildFieldSeries puts the 900.000000 stb into the field oil while counting it as a wellsOn of 0."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Zero is not above zero in either place, and the two consequences are opposite: the rate refuses and the volume is booked with the count against it.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'final', null, 5, 'wellsOn, wellCount, producerCount and the uptime membership are four different counts in reach of one field. Which of them counts wells that produced oil?', '["producerCount, which is what its name states and what excluding the injectors achieves.", "wellsOn, which is the count of rows that met the volume test on the date and is therefore the producing count.", "None of them.", "wellCount, since a series is only handed in for a well that has rows against it."]'::jsonb, 2, 'producerCount excludes injectors only, so an observation well is counted as a producer; wellsOn counts a gas-only row and a water-only row alike; and wellCount is simply the length of the series list.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'final', null, 6, 'An exception on the published well P-5 carries a value of 100.000000000000 against a baseline of 300.000000000000. What are those two numbers?', '["Window means of the oil producing-day rate, which is what the printed stb/d refers to and what the comparison is built on.", "Window means of the calendar oil volume booked on each ledger row.", "The lowest and the highest oil volume the well booked inside the two windows respectively.", "The volumetric oil rates of the two windows, formed by dividing each window''s oil sum by its day count."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine prints "Oil down 67%: 100 vs 300 stb/d baseline." and puts stb/d after two means of calendar volumes.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'final', null, 7, 'How does a returned field watercut of 0.268292682927 relate to the watercuts the four contributing wells each reported?', '["It is a different quantity: the field figure is one division on two sums, so a large well moves it and a small one barely does.", "It is their arithmetic mean, which is what a field watercut means.", "It is their mean weighted by each well''s hours on stream that day.", "It is the largest of the four, since a field watercut is bounded below by its wettest well."]'::jsonb, 0, 'buildFieldSeries adds every row on the date and then divides once, and the mean of the contributing wells'' watercuts is not what the function returns.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'final', null, 8, 'A ledger row books 450.000000 stb of oil with a null hours column, and the point returns a liquid of 540.000000000 stb and a liquidPd of 540.000000000 stb/d. What separates the two claims?', '["Nothing, since both are 540.000000000 and a claim is what the number says.", "The unit only, since the row was open a full day and the two coincide.", "The rounding, since a rate is quoted to nine places and a volume to six.", "One is a measurement of what the row made and the other is an absence, since a null hours column is read as uptime unknown and the volume is left unscaled."]'::jsonb, 3, 'On a constructed row put through both, oilPd is 600.000000000 stb/d for a null hours column and 600.000000000 stb/d for a stated 24.0, and one of those is a measurement while the other is silence.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'final', null, 9, 'The published row of 2025-01-05 books no oil, no water and 120.000000 Mscf of gas over 24.000000 hours. It refuses one thing and answers another off the same gas. Which is which?', '["The gas-oil ratio answers at 120.000000000 scf/stb and the gas producing-day rate refuses.", "Both refuse, since a row with no oil supports neither a ratio nor a rate.", "The gas-oil ratio refuses for want of oil, and the gas producing-day rate answers at 120.000000000 Mscf/d.", "Both answer, the ratio being formed against the liquid of 0.000000000 stb."]'::jsonb, 2, 'gor is null when the row made no oil however much gas it booked, and the gas is measured and the rate it feeds is computed.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'final', null, 10, 'The published well P-3 files 6 points at a cadence of 30.000000 days. What does derivePoint do with each of those rows?', '["It scales each row by the cadence the series reports, which is what seriesCadenceDays is computed for.", "It reads every one of them as a calendar day, because nothing in the input could tell it otherwise.", "It refuses a row whose period exceeds one day, which is why the series carries 6 points and not the 47 its neighbours file.", "It divides each period volume by 30.000000 before forming the rates and ratios."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The function never says how long a row actually covers, and there is no field in the input that carries it.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'final', null, 11, 'On the published well P-1 the recent gas-oil ratio is 1360.267857142857 scf/stb read one way and 1141.902313624679 scf/stb read the other. Which function produced which?', '["detectExceptions produced 1360.267857142857 scf/stb as a mean of daily ratios, and the volumetric reading of 1141.902313624679 scf/stb is the one computeKpis takes.", "computeKpis produced the larger and detectExceptions the smaller.", "Both are detectExceptions returns, one before smoothing and one after.", "Both are computeKpis returns, at windowDays of 7 and of 30."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The rises are 70.033482142857 per cent and 42.737789203085 per cent and the overstatement between them is 19.122961825433 per cent.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'final', null, 12, 'uptimePct and a producing-day rate both come out of an hours column. What is the difference in how they treat a point with no hours recorded?', '["Both treat it as a full twenty-four hours, which is the module''s documented default.", "Both refuse, the rate returning null and the uptime returning null for the whole window.", "The rate refuses and the uptime enters the point at zero hours, which pulls the field figure down.", "The rate falls back to the calendar volume and the uptime counts no slot at all, so the point is silently absent from the second."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Of the 7 series read for uptime on the teaching field, only 5 carry an hours_on on any row, and nothing in the return says which.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'final', null, 13, 'A producer files rows with a null hours column on every day. Where does it appear and where does it not?', '["It is outside both, a null hours column excluding a row from every count in the domain.", "It is inside both, a null being read as a full twenty-four hours in each.", "It is inside the wellsOn count, because null passes the second clause, and outside the uptime, because it contributes no slot.", "It is outside wellsOn and inside the uptime at zero hours."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The wellsOn clause admits a row whose hours_on is null or above zero, and the uptime counts a slot only where the column carries a finite number.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'final', null, 14, 'Two ledger rows go in, one of them carrying no well. What is the one figure that could reconcile the field total against the sum of the well series?', '["The wellsOn count on the field day, which is the number of rows that reached a series and is printed for that purpose.", "There is none, and the difference is exactly the rows nobody attached a well to.", "The wellCount computeKpis returns beside the means, which is the length of the series list and therefore the row audit.", "The difference between the field oil of 1800.000000 stb and the 900.000000 stb the one attached row carried."]'::jsonb, 1, 'buildWellSeries returns 1 series with 1 point where buildFieldSeries returns 1 field day carrying 1800.000000 stb of oil, and neither object says a row went missing.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'final', null, 15, 'A row''s watercut of 0.900000000000 has to be quoted into a lift screening rule. What has to happen to it, and what happens if nothing does?', '["It has to be multiplied to a per cent, and left alone it describes a well at 0.9 per cent water, which is almost dry.", "Nothing, since the two files agree on the convention.", "It has to be divided by a hundred, since screening carries the finer spelling.", "It has to be refused, since a lift rule cannot take a surveillance figure."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A watercut is a 0 to 1 fraction in surveillance.js and allocation.js and a per cent in liftScreening.js and liftAdvisor.js, and nothing at the boundary converts.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'final', null, 16, 'hours_on moves on a row from 24.0 to 12.0. Which of the row''s members move with it?', '["Every computed member doubles, since the scaling is applied to the point after the members are formed.", "The watercut and the gas-oil ratio double and the four rates hold, the ratios being the members the hours column feeds.", "Nothing moves, since derivePoint scales a row only when the hours column is missing.", "The four producing-day rates double and the watercut and the gas-oil ratio do not move at all."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Both terms of each ratio are unscaled volumes off the same row, and scaling both by one multiplier would not have moved them either.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'final', null, 17, 'decimate is held at maxPoints = 1500. Off a 2999-point series it returns 1500 and off a 3001-point series it returns 1001. Which of the two used the larger stride?', '["The 2999-point series, since a larger returned count is what a larger stride produces.", "Neither, since the stride is fixed at 2 for any series between the cap and twice it.", "The 3001-point series, which strides by 3 where the shorter one strides by 2.", "Both used a stride of 3, and the difference is in the always-keep-the-last rule."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The stride is Math.ceil(n / maxPoints), an integer computed once, so two points more on the input can halve the output.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'final', null, 18, 'Two computeKpis returns on the published field carry an identical oil of 3256.666666667 stb/d, an identical watercut and an identical uptime. What differs between them?', '["The number of field days averaged, 48 in the first and 51 in the second, which is why the object states it.", "Only the windowDays they report, 180 in one and 365 in the other, since both averaged the same 51 field days.", "The asOf, since a wider window anchors further back and the two returns are anchored on different dates.", "The two counts, which widen with the window and are the only members that do."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Past 51 days the ledger runs out, and the object reports windowDays as asked rather than as measured.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'final', null, 19, 'In the hours sweep the uplift reaches 48.000000000 at hours_on = 0.5. What is the uplift, and what does that value of it mean?', '["It is the producing-day rate divided by the calendar volume of the same row, so the row is reporting forty-eight times what it booked.", "It is the ratio of the row''s gas to its oil, which the sweep holds fixed.", "It is the fraction of the day the well was open, inverted for printing.", "It is the factor the module applies before refusing the row."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The same constructed row of 600 stb reports 28800.000000000 stb/d at that uplift, which is a correct answer to what the well would make in a day at that rate.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'final', null, 20, 'What has to happen for a rate_drop to be printed at high rather than medium?', '["The well has to raise a second exception in the same window, which is what promotes the first.", "The drop has to be measured on the producing-day rate rather than on the calendar volume.", "The drop has to reach 40 per cent, which is the doubling of the rateDropPct trigger of 20.", "The baseline mean has to be above the minOilRate of 5, which is what admits the well to the top rank."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A severity is the name of a threshold crossing and nothing else, so high, medium and info carry no information about size beyond which crossing was made.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'final', null, 21, 'A capstone field asks for a watercut and the answer is written as a per cent. The expected value is a fraction. How does the grader treat it?', '["As a near miss inside tolerance, since the two differ by a factor of a hundred and the tolerance the grader applies is a relative one.", "As correct after normalisation, since the grader converts both sides to one convention first.", "As a partial credit, scaled by how far the answer sits from the tolerance band.", "As a miss, since it compares an absolute difference in the field''s own units against a tolerance in the same units."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The tolerance is not a percentage of anything, so a watercut carried as a fraction and the same watercut carried as a per cent are two different quantities.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'final', null, 22, 'Over the P-1 baseline both readings give a watercut of 0.352517985612, and over the OGUTA-2 baseline both give a gas-oil ratio of 580.000000000000 scf/stb. What do those two windows have in common?', '["Both are 30-day windows, and a window of that length holds enough rows for the difference between the two readings to average away.", "The daily ratio does not move across either of them, and where it is constant the two readings are one number.", "Both were formed by the same function, which is why no disagreement can appear in either.", "Both sit before the recent window, and a baseline is stored volumetrically in both cases."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The disagreement appears only where the window mixes rates, which is why a window of uniform days cannot show the problem at all.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'final', null, 23, 'A single ledger row of 800 stb of oil and 200 stb of water arrives with both volume columns typed as text. Which of the row''s field-day members survive it?', '["The oil of 800.000000000 stb and the water of 200.000000000 stb survive, and the liquid of 8000200.000000000 stb and the watercut of 0.000024999375 do not.", "None of the four, since a text column corrupts every member downstream of it once the very first row has turned the accumulator into a string rather than a number of its own.", "All four, since the accumulator coerces each string to a number before it is added and the field day therefore carries the same values it would have carried off numeric columns.", "The two ratios survive and the two sums do not, since a division coerces a string back to a number while the two accumulated columns carry the text forward into the field day unchanged."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Only the member formed by adding one string to another is damaged, and nothing in the return says a column arrived as text.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'final', null, 24, 'A constructed row of 600 stb of oil records hours_on as 168.0 and returns an oilPd of 85.714285714 stb/d. What is the giveaway?', '["The rate is not a round number, which a volume scaled by twenty-four over the hours always is when the hours column holds a whole number of them.", "The hours value exceeds 24, which Number.isFinite rejects, so the row should have returned a null rate rather than one scaled off an impossible day.", "The gas-oil ratio on the same row moved with the hours, which is the one member that could not have done so had the column been read correctly.", "The ratio to the calendar volume is 0.142857143, and a producing-day rate below the calendar volume of its own row cannot happen."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Nothing clamps hours_on to twenty-four, so a meter reporting cumulative hours scales the rate downwards and the module prints it without comment.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'final', null, 25, 'computeKpis is handed a field series with no days in it. What does it return, and why is that the useful answer?', '["An object of zeroes, which is what a caller can safely plot without a special case.", "An object with a windowDays and nothing else, so the caller knows what was asked for and can tell an empty call from a quiet field.", "null, because an object of zeroes is the one thing a caller could mistake for a quiet field.", "The previous return for the same asOf, held so that a chart does not blank."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A field day of all zeroes is the other case and it does return an object, carrying a liquid of 0.000000, a null watercut, a null gor and a null uptimePct.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'final', null, 26, 'seriesCadenceDays returns 15.500000 on a series whose gaps are 1, 1, 30 and 30 days. What kind of number is that?', '["The mean of the four gaps, which is what a cadence is defined as.", "The median of an even count of gaps, which averages the two middle ones and can land where no gap ever was.", "The span of the series divided by the number of points in it.", "A rounding of 15 up to the next half day, applied so that every widening rule downstream has a finite multiplier to work with."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The cadence is the number every widening rule in detectExceptions is built on, and a single point returns null rather than a guess.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'final', null, 27, 'derivePoint copies nine of its fourteen keys through unchanged. Which of the copied nine reaches an answer the others do not?', '["hoursOn, which is the only input to the four producing-day rates and is returned exactly as it was handed in, negative values included.", "The date, which fixes the window every mean is taken over and is therefore the copied key that decides which comparison a row ever reaches at all.", "The well type, which decides whether the injection branch runs and is therefore the copied key that settles what a row is compared against downstream.", "The injection column, which the on-count reads and which is therefore the copied key that decides whether a row is counted as on stream on its date."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A constructed row at hours_on = -3.0 returns oilPd null and hoursOn -3, so the rate is protected and the column is not.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'final', null, 28, 'A row books 0.0 stb of oil, 400.0 stb of water and 200.0 Mscf of gas. Which of its two ratios exists?', '["The gas-oil ratio, at 500.000000000 scf/stb, since the row measured both gas and liquid.", "Both, the watercut at 1.000000000000 and the gas-oil ratio at 500.000000000 scf/stb.", "The watercut, at 1.000000000000, the liquid of 400.000000 stb being above zero.", "Neither, since a row with no oil supports no ratio of any kind."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A watercut needs liquid and a gas-oil ratio needs oil, so the two refuse on different conditions and a row can easily carry one and not the other.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'final', null, 29, 'Two computeKpis calls on identical rows at different windowDays return a different oil, a different watercut and a different uptime. What has been measured?', '["A real change in the field between the two windows, which is what makes a KPI comparison worth printing.", "The disagreement between the volumetric reading and the mean of daily ratios, which widens with the window.", "The number of field days each window actually reached, which is the quantity the two returns differ in.", "The windows, and not the field."]'::jsonb, 3, 'computeKpis has no baseline in it, so the function that compares two windows on purpose is detectExceptions, and it is a different call with a different convention inside it.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'final', null, 30, 'Smoothing a monthly well at a windowDays of 7 returns the six input values unchanged. What happened?', '["The function silently refused, since a window shorter than the cadence cannot be formed and the input is returned as a documented fallback.", "Nothing went wrong: at a cadence of 30.000000 days every 7-day window holds one point, and the average of one point is that point.", "The series was decimated before smoothing, so the six points that came back are the six that survived the stride.", "The window was read as a count of points rather than a span of days, so six points could not fill a window of seven."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Nothing on the chart says the smoothing did nothing, and the final point of that series reads 11600 stb at 7 days and 22600 stb at 200 days.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'final', null, 31, 'Both detectExceptions and computeKpis anchor on the field''s latest ledger date. What does that buy?', '["A dataset from three years ago surveils honestly instead of declaring every well stale, and the return states the anchor as asOf.", "A guarantee that both functions read the same rows, since anchoring on one date is what makes two window definitions select one set of ledger rows.", "A window that always holds the number of days it asked for, since the anchor is a real ledger date and counting back from it cannot run past the data.", "A comparison that cannot be moved by changing the settings, since the anchor is taken off the data rather than from the settings object that carries the two window lengths."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The published field returns an asOf of 2025-06-30, its own latest ledger date, and every window is measured back from there.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'final', null, 32, 'On the teaching well OGUTA-2, one recent day books 82.000000 stb of oil, 231.000000 stb of water and 141.000000 Mscf of gas and reads a gor of 1719.512195121951 scf/stb against an ordinary day''s 580.357142857143. What does that establish?', '["That gas broke out of solution below the bubble point, which is the only mechanism that moves a gas-oil ratio as far as that pair does.", "That the gas rate roughly tripled between the two days.", "That the denominator fell faster than the numerator, the oil by 12.292682926829 and the gas by 4.148936170213.", "That the water is displacing gas, since it fell by only 1.350649350649 over the same pair while the gas fell by 4.148936170213."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A ratio cannot tell you which of its two terms did it, and reading a gas-oil ratio rise as a statement about gas is the mistake.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'final', null, 33, 'A spreadsheet hands the hours column in as the string "12" on a row of 600 stb of oil. Which module reads it as twelve hours?', '["surveillance only, which coerces a numeric string before testing it and returns 1200.000000000 stb/d.", "allocation only, which reads any non-empty hours cell as a stated uptime.", "Both, since a numeric string is a number once the column is read off the row.", "Neither surveillance nor allocation, since both call Number.isFinite and a string fails it in both."]'::jsonb, 3, 'surveillance returns an oilPd of 600.000000000 stb/d off that row, the same as for a blank column, and nothing anywhere reports that the column was text.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'final', null, 34, 'What can this tier''s two functions never produce, however the call is made?', '["A ratio, which needs the two windows detectExceptions puts side by side before either function can form one.", "A verdict, since neither of them holds a second window to compare against.", "A rate, since scaling to twenty-four hours belongs to the comparison half of the domain.", "A count, since counting wells needs a well type and neither function is handed one."]'::jsonb, 1, 'derivePoint sees one row and computeKpis sees one window, so both return numbers and a reader who wants a well worth looking at has to get it elsewhere.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'final', null, 35, 'An injector books 3000 stb of water into a field day and a well typed observation books nothing at all on the same date. What does buildFieldSeries do with the two rows?', '["It takes both, since it filters on nothing and never asks what type a well is, adding the injection to winj and nothing to oil, water or gas.", "It drops both, injectors and observation wells being outside the field totals, since the function reads the well type off each row before it sums anything.", "It takes the injector and drops the observation row.", "It drops the injector and takes the observation row into the on-count, since a row of zeroes still records a well that was present on the date and reporting."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The injector contributes a wellsOn of 0 because injection is not one of the three columns the volume clause adds.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'final', null, 36, 'The teaching field''s uptime reads 92.011904762 per cent with its observation well and 90.014880952 per cent without. What does that pair price?', '["The difference between the volumetric reading and the daily-ratio reading of an uptime, which is the seam that runs through the rest of this domain.", "The 7-day window against the 30-day window on the same field.", "What a well that produces nothing and records a perfect 24 hours a day is worth to a field availability figure, 1.997023810 points.", "The injector, which the uptime skips and which the second of the two figures adds back so that the pair prices what an injector is worth to a field figure."]'::jsonb, 2, 'computeKpis skips injectors from the uptime and reads everything else, and there is no membership figure anywhere in the returned object.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'final', null, 37, 'On the teaching well OGUTA-6 the engine prints a rate drop of 39 per cent while the producing-day oil rate holds at 512.000000000000 stb/d. Which quantity did the printed exception measure?', '["The producing-day rate, whose fall of 1.856763925729 per cent the printed figure rounds up.", "The hours column, which fell by 39.940476190 per cent and is what the message reports.", "The liquid, which carries the water the oil column does not.", "The calendar volume, whose window means are 307.504761904762 stb and 502.666666666667 stb."]'::jsonb, 3, 'detectExceptions sets its rate key to the calendar volume, so the well is reported as a rate problem while the producing-day rate rose by 1.856763925729 per cent.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'final', null, 38, 'A field day''s watercut and a row''s watercut are formed by the same expression on different inputs. What is the relation between the two?', '["The field figure is the mean of the row figures on that date by construction, each well entering the average once whatever volume it happened to book.", "None that a reader can assume: the field figure is water over liquid on the summed columns, and it is not an average of the row figures.", "The field figure is the row figure of the largest well on the date.", "The field figure is the row figure smoothed over the trailing window, which is what movingAverage is called for on the way into the field series."]'::jsonb, 1, 'buildFieldSeries sums first and divides once, so a large well moves the field figure and a small one barely does.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'final', null, 39, 'uptimePct is quoted as field availability. What does the returned object say about which wells stood behind it?', '["Nothing: there is no slot count, no well list and no membership figure anywhere in it.", "It reports a slot count beside the uptime, which is what makes the figure auditable against the wells that filled the column in.", "It reports the 5 as the producerCount and the 7 as the wellCount.", "It reports a null uptime whenever a series contributed no slot."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The uptime is a mean over an unstated membership: injectors out, observation wells in, and any well that left the column blank silently excluded.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'final', null, 40, 'Why does naming the function matter more in this domain than quoting the number?', '["Because the four files carry different version numbers, and a number is only valid against the one of them it was produced under and no other.", "Because a number quoted without a function cannot be checked against the golden, which indexes every committed value by the function name that produced it.", "Because two functions in one file form the same two ratios two different ways and the disagreement moves a printed severity.", "Because the studio calls the four files in an order that changes what each one sees."]'::jsonb, 2, 'On the published well P-1 the choice between the two readings is the whole difference between a printed high and a printed medium.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'final', null, 41, 'A ledger row carries a stated hours_on of 0. What do surveillance.js and allocation.js each make of it?', '["surveillance returns a null oilPd and allocation reads the zero as a full day on, giving the well its whole share.", "Both read it as uptime unknown and leave the volume unscaled.", "surveillance returns an oilPd of 800.000000000 stb/d off a 800 stb row and allocation returns a theoretical oil of 1000.000000000 stb, the zero reading as unknown in both.", "surveillance returns a null oilPd and allocation gives that well a theoretical oil of 0.000000000 stb, so the two agree that the well was shut."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A stated zero is the one spelling the two modules agree on, and filling a blank hours column with a zero to tidy the data costs that well its whole allocated share for the day.'),
  ('surveillance', 'beginner', 'final', null, 42, 'A number is quoted out of this domain with nothing attached to it. What is missing?', '["The tolerance the grader would apply to it, which is the only thing a bare number lacks once its unit has been written down beside it.", "Which function produced it, over which window, and under which of the several conventions that form the same quantity.", "The severity it would have been printed at.", "The unit, which is the whole of what a bare number is missing once the quantity it names has been stated alongside it in the same sentence."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Every number in this tier is a reading of a period made by a named function under a stated convention, and the number alone does not carry any of the three.');
